1
00:00:01,290 --> 00:00:06,327
Hi again! As promised, we're going to
discuss user testing and feedback.

2
00:00:06,327 --> 00:00:11,282
If the end goal of our project is to have
a great quality product or service for

3
00:00:11,282 --> 00:00:14,225
both our own organization and
our customer,

4
00:00:14,225 --> 00:00:18,350
we need to get an idea of
what the customer wants.

5
00:00:18,350 --> 00:00:21,954
We already learned how to make sure
something is high quality on our end.

6
00:00:21,954 --> 00:00:26,310
Now let's find out how to measure what
clients want, so we can meet their needs,

7
00:00:26,310 --> 00:00:28,160
expectations, and standards.

8
00:00:29,500 --> 00:00:34,307
The best way to get an idea of what
customers and users want is to ask them,

9
00:00:34,307 --> 00:00:37,440
but we don't mean calling each
of them up on the phone and

10
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:41,049
literally asking them. That might
not be the best use of our time.

11
00:00:41,049 --> 00:00:45,220
Fortunately, there are a few ways
we can streamline that information.

12
00:00:45,220 --> 00:00:49,965
We can conduct a series of surveys or
tests with customers and users.

13
00:00:49,965 --> 00:00:54,531
Some common ones are feedback surveys and
user acceptance tests.

14
00:00:54,531 --> 00:00:56,404
Let's start with feedback surveys.

15
00:00:56,404 --> 00:01:01,297
Feedback surveys are a survey in which
users provide feedback on features of

16
00:01:01,297 --> 00:01:03,903
your product that they like or dislike.

17
00:01:03,903 --> 00:01:06,382
These surveys can take
place as you design,

18
00:01:06,382 --> 00:01:10,951
before you launch, in order to find out if
people like and understand the product,

19
00:01:10,951 --> 00:01:15,436
or after you've launched, if you want to
make sure the user experience is even

20
00:01:15,436 --> 00:01:16,570
more satisfying.

21
00:01:17,900 --> 00:01:19,961
So your users participate in a survey and

22
00:01:19,961 --> 00:01:22,880
give you feedback on what
features they like or dislike,

23
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:26,620
and potentially, which aspects
they find to be fairly intuitive,

24
00:01:26,620 --> 00:01:28,945
and which aspects are a little
tougher to navigate.

25
00:01:28,945 --> 00:01:33,461
After you get that feedback, you'll either
be good to launch, if you haven't yet,

26
00:01:33,461 --> 00:01:37,605
or you'll go back and iterate on
the product, if it's already on the market.

27
00:01:37,605 --> 00:01:41,900
Alternatively, you might
conduct user acceptance tests.

28
00:01:41,900 --> 00:01:46,616
In broader terms, a user acceptance test,
or a UAT, is a test that helps

29
00:01:46,616 --> 00:01:51,430
a business make sure that a product or
solution works for its users.

30
00:01:51,430 --> 00:01:54,421
A UAT must meet the agreed
upon requirements and

31
00:01:54,421 --> 00:01:56,450
deliver the expected results.

32
00:01:56,450 --> 00:02:00,920
This test is typically used to
assess the end-to-end experience for

33
00:02:00,920 --> 00:02:03,448
the user of a new process or product.

34
00:02:03,448 --> 00:02:07,987
A user acceptance test is incredibly
important because it takes place near

35
00:02:07,987 --> 00:02:10,083
the end of a product's development,

36
00:02:10,083 --> 00:02:14,317
and therefore, is an overall user
experience test of the entire product,

37
00:02:14,317 --> 00:02:15,546
software, or service.

38
00:02:15,546 --> 00:02:19,452
UATs are sometimes
referred to as "beta tests."

39
00:02:19,452 --> 00:02:23,460
Let's find out what a user acceptance
test agenda might look like.

40
00:02:23,460 --> 00:02:27,044
In a typical UAT setting,
you'll welcome your users and

41
00:02:27,044 --> 00:02:29,069
thank them for participating.

42
00:02:29,069 --> 00:02:31,692
Then, you'll present the product to them.

43
00:02:31,692 --> 00:02:34,618
This includes discussing
testing guidelines and

44
00:02:34,618 --> 00:02:36,923
demonstrating how the product works.

45
00:02:36,923 --> 00:02:39,594
Next, you'll start your UAT test cases,

46
00:02:39,594 --> 00:02:43,092
taking your audience through
critical user journeys.

47
00:02:43,092 --> 00:02:48,031
A critical user journey is a sequence
of steps a user follows to accomplish

48
00:02:48,031 --> 00:02:49,633
tasks in your product.

49
00:02:49,633 --> 00:02:54,137
When presenting something you've built,
you must give users a visual

50
00:02:54,137 --> 00:02:58,732
representation or mock up of your
product or have them go through a demo.

51
00:02:58,732 --> 00:03:02,276
For example, if you're working on
a construction-based project and

52
00:03:02,276 --> 00:03:05,513
you intend to replace all appliances and
hardware in the home,

53
00:03:05,513 --> 00:03:09,133
you'll want to give the user some sort
of vision of what that might entail.

54
00:03:09,133 --> 00:03:14,732
This could include 3D models,
digital blueprints, samples, and more.

55
00:03:14,732 --> 00:03:18,264
Your UAT demo should focus
on a call to action.

56
00:03:18,264 --> 00:03:20,474
For instance, the call to action for

57
00:03:20,474 --> 00:03:24,833
your project may be the need to test
hardware in the client's future home.

58
00:03:24,833 --> 00:03:28,869
Maybe the homeowners have requested a
dishwasher that can be opened and

59
00:03:28,869 --> 00:03:32,453
closed with very little force and
doesn't make too much noise.

60
00:03:32,453 --> 00:03:33,359
In that case,

61
00:03:33,359 --> 00:03:37,742
you'll want to give the client real-life scenarios to work within.

62
00:03:37,742 --> 00:03:40,879
Ask them to load the dishes and
start the wash cycle.

63
00:03:40,879 --> 00:03:45,900
Then ask questions like, "On a scale of
one to 10, how much force was required to

64
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:51,486
open and close the dishwasher?" to determine if the washer meets their expectations.

65
00:03:51,486 --> 00:03:54,387
During your presentation and
walkthrough of the UAT,

66
00:03:54,387 --> 00:03:58,911
you should be collecting feedback from
the users on their overall experience.

67
00:03:58,911 --> 00:04:00,624
During this part of testing,

68
00:04:00,624 --> 00:04:04,598
your users will be able to help you
identify edge cases. Edge cases

69
00:04:04,598 --> 00:04:08,721
are rare—outliers that the original
requirements didn't account for.

70
00:04:08,721 --> 00:04:13,152
They deal with the extreme maximums and
minimums of parameters.

71
00:04:13,152 --> 00:04:16,704
For example, imagine that you
created an app that allows for

72
00:04:16,704 --> 00:04:18,306
unlimited photo uploads,

73
00:04:18,306 --> 00:04:23,331
knowing that users will rarely upload more
than 1000 photos in a single session.

74
00:04:23,331 --> 00:04:27,917
How will the system deal with someone
who actually does upload thousands of

75
00:04:27,917 --> 00:04:30,332
photos, or millions, in a single upload?

76
00:04:30,332 --> 00:04:33,751
It's unlikely, but
it could be disastrous for your software.

77
00:04:33,751 --> 00:04:38,471
After identifying edge cases, the last
step of the UAT agenda is to recap

78
00:04:38,471 --> 00:04:41,431
your findings, identify bugs or issues, and

79
00:04:41,431 --> 00:04:45,040
prioritize which issues
need to be addressed first.

80
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:48,546
When you've addressed the issue and
determined next steps,

81
00:04:48,546 --> 00:04:52,403
you'll be able close and
conclude your user acceptance testing.

82
00:04:52,403 --> 00:04:54,033
And there you have it!

83
00:04:54,033 --> 00:04:57,958
So we've learned a bit more about the
importance of listening to feedback from

84
00:04:57,958 --> 00:04:58,619
customers

85
00:04:58,619 --> 00:05:03,194
and discussed some common methods for
measuring customer satisfaction, like

86
00:05:03,194 --> 00:05:06,793
feedback surveys,
user acceptance tests, and edge cases.

87
00:05:06,793 --> 00:05:08,072
That's a lot to learn.

88
00:05:08,072 --> 00:05:08,893
Keep it up!

89
00:05:08,893 --> 00:05:10,798
You're doing great so far!

90
00:05:10,798 --> 00:05:14,885
In our next video, we'll pivot to
identifying reasons why risks and

91
00:05:14,885 --> 00:05:18,014
changes might occur and
how to manage dependencies.

92
00:05:18,014 --> 00:05:18,690
I'll see you soon.
